  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday through Thursday: The Inland NW will be under the\ninfluence of an upper trough resulting in a cool and showery\nweather pattern. A deep closed low dropping out of southern\nBritish Columbia will bring cooler temperatures to the region,\nalong with increasing showers as the mid level become more\nunstable as 500mb temperatures plunge Tuesday Night and\nWednesday to -30C over the northern half of Washington into the\nNorth Idaho Panhandle. This combined with lower level upslope\nflow from west-southwest winds at 850mb into the Cascade crest,\nand mountains of Northeast Washington and the North ID Panhandle\nwill produce several inches of snow. Bands of convergence into\nthe Cascades also has the potential to produce brief periods of\nheavier snow with HREF showing around a 30-40 percent chance of\n1 inch per hour snow rates Tuesday Night into Wednesday morning.\nA Winter Weather Advisory remains in place for elevations above\n3000 feet in the Cascades from 5 PM Tuesday to 5 PM Wednesday with\nthe highest amounts expected over Stevens and Washington \nPasses. Wednesday Night into Thursday as the closed low drops\nsouth to off the WA coast the flow turns southwest. And some\nsubtle mid level drying will result in a decrease in the\nintensity and coverage of showers, but enough moisture lingers\nover the northern mountains and Cascades for additional light\nrain and mountain snow amounts. Below are the NBM chances for \nvarious snow thresholds being reached for the mountain passes \nfrom 5 PM Tuesday to 5 PM Thursday, which have increased \ncompared to yesterday's probabilities:\n\nMountain Pass   2+ inches   4+ inches   6+ inches   8+ inches\nStevens Pass    100%        95%         80%         50%\nWashington Pass 100%        70%         35%         10%     \nSherman Pass     90%        40%          1%          0% \nLookout Pass     90%        50%          0%          0% \n\nFriday through Monday: The influence of the upper low continues Friday \nand Saturday as another wave associated with the low passes\nthrough for more cool and showery conditions with mountain snow\nshowers, and a rain/snow mix in the valleys. On Sunday the low \nstarts to push out and is replaced by an upper ridge on Monday\nbringing dry weather and slightly warmer temperatures.",
